<Section id="first-shape" title="1. Draw a Shape with Data">

Up to now we typed every number ourselves. Let's load some **real data** instead — a list of popular songs from the 2010s — and let the data decide what we draw.

We pull the data in with an `import`. It loads the file once and gives the list a name we can use everywhere - `data`:

```svelte
/// file: Load the data
<script>
	import data from '$assets/data/spotify-2010s.csv';

	console.log(data);
</script>
```

`console.log(...)` prints a value to the browser's **console** so we can look at it (open it with **F12**, then the _Console_ tab). Logging `data` shows an **array** — a numbered list — with one entry per song.

Each entry is a single song. To grab the first one we use its position in the list. Positions start at **0**, so the first song is `data[0]`. We give it a name so it's easy to reuse - this name is called a **variable**:

```svelte
/// file: Name the first row
let row = data[0];
console.log(row);
```

`row` contains several named fields. We read a field by writing the name after a dot - `row.title`, `row.artist`, `row.popularity`:

```svelte
/// file: Read its fields
console.log(row.title); // "Hey, Soul Sister"
console.log(row.popularity); // 83
```

So far these values only appear in the console. To put one on the **page**, we wrap it in **curly braces** `{ }` right in the markup. Whatever sits between the braces is run as JavaScript and its result is dropped into the drawing — either as an attribute value or as text on screen:

```svelte
/// file: Curly braces put a value into the markup
<circle r={row.popularity} />  <!-- attribute: no quotes around the braces -->
<text>{row.title}</text>       <!-- text: braces between the tags -->
```

Now we can **draw with it**. We size a circle by the song's `popularity` and label it with the `title` and `artist`. In the exercise the shape is drawn with the values typed in by hand — swap each one for the matching field on `row` so the drawing comes from the data:

<Exercise file="5-draw-a-shape-with-data.svelte">
	<DrawAShapeWithData />
</Exercise>

{#snippet sidebar()}

**Open the console**

`console.log` writes to your browser's console. Open it with **F12** (or right-click the page → _Inspect_ → _Console_).

**What you should see**

```js
/// file: Console
// console.log(data)
Array(139) [ {…}, {…}, … ]   

// console.log(row)     
{ title: "Hey, Soul Sister", … }  

// console.log(row.title)
"Hey, Soul Sister"       

// console.log(row.popularity)         
83                                
```

> [!Note] New words
> - **array**: a numbered list. `data` is an array of 139 songs.
> - **index**: a spot in the list, counting from `0`. `data[0]` is the first song.
> - **variable**: a name for a value: `let row = data[0]`.

**Size the circle by the data**

```svelte
/// file: Circle
<circle cx="150" cy="150" r={row.popularity} fill="steelblue" />
```


```svelte
/// file: Filling the text
<text x="150" y="240" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="bold" fill="#222">
	{row.title}
</text>
```

**RESULT**:

